        <h2 id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          1. What Teak Oil Actually Is
        </h2>

        <p>
          It is not oil from teak. "Teak oil" is a category name describing what a product is for, not what it contains, and formulations vary enormously between brands. Most are a blend of <strong>linseed or tung oil</strong> thinned with solvents so it penetrates dense wood, and the better ones add <strong>resins</strong> for durability and <strong>UV absorbers</strong> to slow fading.
        </p>

        <p>
          That variability matters when comparing products. A cheap teak oil may be little more than thinned linseed oil; a premium one contains polymers that leave a more durable finish and absorbers that meaningfully extend the colour. Two cans on the same shelf with the same name can behave quite differently, which is why the reviews above focus on what each actually does rather than on the label.
        </p>

        <p>
          What all of them share is the mechanism. Oil penetrates the wood fibres, darkens and enriches the natural colour, and leaves a low sheen that reads as wood rather than as coating. Nothing builds on the surface, so nothing peels — the finish wears away gradually and is repaired by wiping on more oil, which is genuinely one of the pleasanter jobs in furniture care.
        </p>

        <h2 id="oil-vs-sealer" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          2. Oil vs Sealer: The Decision That Matters
        </h2>

        <p>
          Almost everyone searching for teak oil is actually choosing between two categories, and the right answer depends on one thing: ultraviolet light.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Pigment is what blocks UV.</strong> A pigmented teak sealer carries fine particles that physically block and scatter ultraviolet light before it reaches the lignin that carries teak's colour. That is why sealers hold colour for six to twelve months outdoors.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Oil mostly does not carry pigment.</strong> Its UV defence comes from absorbers, which work by being consumed, so their protection tapers. Outdoors in full sun, expect a plain oil to start flattening at around three months and want a refresh by six.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is a second, less-discussed difference: oil is organic, and in damp, shaded conditions it gives surface mould something to feed on. Teak that has been generously oiled and then stored under a tight cover through a wet winter frequently comes out speckled black. Marine oils with mildew inhibitors exist precisely because this is a real problem.
        </p>

        <p>
          So the honest recommendation splits cleanly. <strong>Interior teak: oil</strong>, every time — there is no UV to speak of, mildew is not a factor, and nothing else makes a mid-century sideboard look as good. <strong>Exterior teak: a pigmented sealer</strong>, unless you actively enjoy the maintenance rhythm and want the richest possible colour in exchange for it. Our <a href="/teak-oil-vs-teak-sealer/">teak oil vs teak sealer comparison</a> works through the trade-off in detail, and our <a href="/best-teak-sealer/">teak sealer reviews</a> cover the alternative.
        </p>

