        <p>
          One practical note on buying: teak oil goes much further than the tin suggests, because dense teak accepts very little per coat. A single quart will cover a large set of garden furniture with two coats and still have some left, so the sensible purchase is the smallest size that covers the job rather than the value-sized tin. Half-used oil also skins over in storage, which makes the bigger container a false economy on anything but a boat's worth of brightwork.
        </p>

        <h2 id="indoors-outdoors" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          4. Indoors vs Outdoors
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Interior Furniture</h3>
        <p>
          This is where teak oil belongs. A dining table, a sideboard, a mid-century chair — all benefit from a finish that penetrates rather than coats, feels like wood under the hand, and can be repaired in minutes with a rag. Two or three thin coats initially, then a refresh once or twice a year, keeps interior teak looking exactly as it should indefinitely.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Garden Furniture</h3>
        <p>
          Here the oil-versus-sealer question bites hardest. A set of teak chairs in full sun will lose its oiled colour within a season, and the honest choice is between refreshing every few months or accepting a pigmented sealer with less immediate richness and far longer intervals. Neither is wrong; only the pretence that oil holds colour outdoors is.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Boat Trim and Brightwork</h3>
        <p>
          Marine oils with mildew inhibitors are worth the premium here, because a covered boat is exactly the damp, dark environment where an ordinary oil feeds mould. Oil also keeps the surface matte and grippy underfoot, which is why plenty of owners prefer it to varnish on anything walked on.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weathered Grey Teak</h3>
        <p>
          Oil will darken silvered teak, but it will not restore it — the grey is degraded surface fibre, and putting oil over it gives a muddy, uneven result. The sequence that works is to clean with a two-part teak cleaner and brightener first, let the wood come back to a light honey colour, allow a full day or two of drying, and only then oil.
        </p>

        <h2 id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          5. How to Apply Teak Oil
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Clean and dry first.</strong> Two-part cleaner and brightener on weathered wood, a simple wash on wood that is merely dusty. Then a minimum of 24 hours drying, longer in humid weather — oil cannot penetrate wet fibres.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Apply thin, with a rag or brush.</strong> Work a section at a time, along the grain. Teak is dense and takes far less than the tin suggests, so resist the urge to flood it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Wipe back the excess.</strong> After ten to fifteen minutes, wipe off everything that has not absorbed. This is the step that separates a beautiful finish from a sticky one — surplus oil left to dry cures into a tacky film that collects dust for weeks.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Build two or three coats.</strong> Thin coats, following the recoat time on the tin. More coats give more depth, but there is a limit: beyond three or four, you are building surface rather than feeding wood.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Give it a settled dry spell outdoors.</strong> Rain or heavy dew on partially cured oil leaves a blotchy finish, and dew is easier to forget than rain. Morning application on a dry day beats an evening one.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sanding: Usually Unnecessary</h3>
        <p>
          People reach for sandpaper far too readily with teak. A two-part cleaner and brightener does chemically what sanding does mechanically, without removing wood, and teak's naturally silica-rich surface is worth keeping rather than abrading away. On a piece that has simply gone grey and dull, cleaning is the whole preparation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sanding earns its place in two situations. The first is raised, furry grain after a piece has been left bare for years and repeatedly wetted — a light pass at 120 to 150 grit along the grain knocks that down and makes the surface pleasant to touch again. The second is a previous film finish that is flaking, where the remnants have to come off before oil can penetrate at all. In both cases work with the grain, keep the grit fine, and stop as soon as the surface is even.
        </p>

        <h2 id="rag-safety" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          6. The Rag Safety Warning
        </h2>

        <p>
          This deserves its own section because it is genuinely dangerous and almost never mentioned on the shelf.
        </p>

        <p>
          Oil finishes cure by oxidation, and oxidation produces heat. A rag soaked in teak oil and left crumpled in a pile traps that heat, the temperature rises, and the process accelerates as it warms. Under the right conditions a bin of oily rags can reach ignition temperature entirely on its own, with no spark and no flame involved. House fires start this way every year.
        </p>

        <p>
          The prevention is simple and takes seconds. <strong>Spread used rags out flat</strong> — over the edge of a bin, on a concrete path, weighted on a wall — outdoors, away from anything combustible, until they are completely dry and stiff. At that point the oxidation is finished and they are inert. <strong>Or submerge them in water</strong> in a sealed metal container until you can dispose of them.
        </p>

        <p>
          What you must never do is ball them up in a bin liner, leave them in a bucket in the garage, or put them in the household waste while still wet with oil. Applies equally to brushes, applicator pads, and the old T-shirt you used to wipe back the excess.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Pro Tip: Decide Where the Rags Go Before You Start</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
            Put a bucket of water outside the door before opening the tin, and drop every rag straight into it as you finish with it. The risk only materialises when tidying up happens in a hurry at the end of a long job, and removing the decision entirely is the most reliable fix.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keeping Oiled Teak Looking Good</h3>
        <p>
          The rhythm matters more than the product. A quick rinse and a soft brush along the grain every few weeks through the season keeps pollen, food and bird droppings from staining, and takes two minutes. Wipe up oily spills promptly — sun cream and salad dressing are the two things most likely to leave a dark mark on an oiled table.
        </p>
        <p>
          Refresh the surfaces that need it rather than the whole piece. Horizontal areas take almost all of the sun and wear roughly twice as fast as the legs and rails beneath them, so a mid-season wipe of oil over the table top and seat slats keeps the colour even without redoing everything. Once a year, clean the whole piece back with a teak cleaner, let it dry properly, and re-oil from a clean start.
        </p>
        <p>
          Over winter, a breathable cover extends any finish considerably, but airflow is the condition. A tight, unventilated cover traps condensation against oiled wood and produces exactly the black mildew speckling the cover was bought to prevent — the single most common way a well-oiled set of furniture comes out of storage looking worse than it went in.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Oily Rags in a Pile</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oxidising oil generates heat, and a crumpled pile of rags can self-ignite. Spread them flat outdoors until stiff and dry, or submerge them in water in a sealed metal container. This is the most important line on the page.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting Oil to Stop Outdoor Greying</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Without pigment, ultraviolet light keeps working on the wood underneath. Plain oil in full sun holds colour for months, not seasons. If that timescale does not suit you, buy a pigmented sealer instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Oiling Over Grey Weathered Wood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The silver layer is degraded fibre, and oil over it goes muddy and uneven. Clean and brighten first, let the honey colour return, dry fully, and then oil.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Wiping Back the Excess</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Teak takes far less oil than the tin implies. Anything not absorbed within fifteen minutes cures tacky and collects dust for weeks. Wipe it all back; the wood has taken what it needs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recoating Endlessly Without Cleaning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coat over coat without ever letting the finish wear back gradually darkens the wood and builds a gummy surface. Clean back once a year with a teak cleaner, let it lighten, then re-oil.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best teak oil?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Star brite Premium Golden Teak Oil gives the richest colour and lasts longer outdoors thanks to added polymers and UV absorbers. For interior furniture, Watco Teak Oil Finish is the classic hand-rubbed choice, and TotalBoat's marine oil adds the mildew inhibitors that matter on a covered boat.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is teak oil made from teak?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">No — it is a category name, not an ingredient. Most products blend linseed or tung oil with solvents, often with resins and UV absorbers added. Formulations differ considerably between brands, which is why two cans with the same name can behave quite differently.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does teak furniture need oiling?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">No. Teak's natural oils and silica keep it rot-resistant with no finish at all, and untreated teak simply silvers. Oiling is purely about appearance — it restores the honey colour temporarily and does nothing for the wood's lifespan.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you apply it?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Every three to six months outdoors in full sun, because plain oils carry no pigment to block UV. Indoors, once or twice a year is plenty. If that outdoor rhythm sounds tiring, a pigmented teak sealer holds colour several times longer.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Oil or sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Outdoors, a pigmented sealer usually wins — pigment physically blocks UV and holds colour six to twelve months rather than three to six. Teak oil gives a richer immediate look and is the better choice indoors, where UV is not a factor and appearance is the point.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can teak oil cause mildew?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">In damp, shaded conditions, yes — oil is organic and gives surface mould something to feed on, which is why oiled teak stored under a tight cover often comes out speckled black. Marine formulations with mildew inhibitors are the answer where that risk is real.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Are oily rags really a fire risk?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, genuinely. Oil finishes cure by oxidation, which generates heat, and a crumpled pile of rags can reach ignition temperature on its own with no spark involved. Spread them flat outdoors until dry and stiff, or submerge them in water in a sealed metal container.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you oil over old oil?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, if the surface is clean and the previous coats have worn back rather than built up. Endless recoating without cleaning gradually darkens the wood and leaves a gummy surface — clean with a teak cleaner once a year, let the wood lighten, then re-oil.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

